<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.9.2: Graphing Inequalities</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00508</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.9.2: Graphing Inequalities</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1f7e5157-ecc8-446c-bf8d-6ce2b150dccd</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes) </title>
<para>Students will work to match inequalities with their graphs. Encourage students to read each inequality aloud and identify some numbers are less than or greater than the number in the inequality.
</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Put students into pairs.
</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Work with a partner to match each of the following inequalities to its correct number line.</para>

<!--Q1-->
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> <figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is a closed circle on 2. The number line to the right of 2 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/54abcd7f180a4a231f0c56939b9b80cb59e7937c"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\( x ≥ 2 \).</para>
<!--Q2-->
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> <figure><media alt=" A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 2. The number line to the left of 2 is highlighted.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2f4fe0101da319082dafcc0d4270b658c7896b6c"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\( x &lt; 2 \).</para>
<!--Q3-->
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> <figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 2. The number line to the right of 2 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/eb89a6cc2c34dfae83f6532ba5c78296deda7a46"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\( x &gt; 2 \).</para>
<!--Q4-->
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> <figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is a closed circle on 2. The number line to the left of 2 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e0a6a95355863d5297d304b9ef6e7e16b7b75709"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\( x ≤ 2 \).</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>As a class, facilitate a discussion about the direction of the shaded arrow and the closed vs. open circle. 
</para>
<title>2.9.2 Self Check </title>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>Following the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></emphasis>
</para>
<newline/><!--SELF CHECK QUESTION GOES BEFORE THE Table -->

<para>QUESTION:</para>
<para>Which number line is represented by the inequality \(x ≥ -4\)?</para>

<!--SELF CHECK table-->
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Answers</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Feedback</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on –4. The number line to the right of –4 is highlighted.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6427b9f2ed4f8fbd52eaedf1352400ea81982bd3"/></media></figure></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The symbol \(≥\) is represented by a closed circle. The answer is <figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 2. The number line to the left of 2 is highlighted.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f49cc596b988d76b1047cc15bac9e54e4543f52a"/></media></figure></para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is a closed circle on –4. The number line to the right of –4 is highlighted.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f49cc596b988d76b1047cc15bac9e54e4543f52a"/></media></figure></entry>
      <entry><para>That’s correct! Check
          yourself: There is a closed circle on \(-4\). The
          number line to the right of \(-4\) is highlighted.</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is a closed circle on –4. The number line to the left of –4 is highlighted.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d8f69d6ab14383cbe8da94de769948f80e16a807"/></media></figure></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try
          again a different way: The number line to the right should be highlighted
          instead of the left. The answer is <figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 2. The number line to the left of 2 is highlighted.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f49cc596b988d76b1047cc15bac9e54e4543f52a"/></media></figure></para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para><figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on –4. The number line to the left of –4 is "><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/795f4d64f9c13b481c4537a92e3038f4ac3be9bc"/></media></figure></para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The symbol \(≥\) is represented by a closed circle. The number line to the right should be
          highlighted instead of the left. The answer is <figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 2. The number line to the left of 2 is highlighted.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f49cc596b988d76b1047cc15bac9e54e4543f52a"/></media></figure></para></entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>


<title>2.9.2: Additional Resources</title>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it. </emphasis></emphasis>
</para>
<title>Graphing Linear Inequalities</title>
<para>What number would make the inequality \( x &gt; 3 \) true? Are you thinking, “\(x\) could be four”? That’s correct, but \(x\) could be 6, too, or 37, or even 3.001. Any number greater than 3 is a solution to the inequality \( x &gt; 3 \). We show all the solutions to the inequality \(x &gt; 3\) on the number line by shading in all the numbers to the right of 3, to show that all numbers greater than 3 are solutions. Because the number 3 itself is not a solution, we put an open dot at 3.</para>
<para>We can also represent inequalities using interval notation. There is no upper end to the solution to this inequality. In interval notation, we express \( x &gt; 3 \) as \((3, ∞)\). The symbol ∞ is read as “infinity.” It is not an actual number.</para>
<para>The figure below shows both the number line and the interval notation.</para>
<para>                                                                          \( x &gt; 3 \)</para>
<para><figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 3. The number line to the right of 3 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1f11f9f52563f8687b96d97d1f8d166c69c5b4d7"/></media></figure></para>
<para>The inequality \(x≤1\) means all numbers less than or equal to 1. Here we need to show that 1 is a solution, too. We do that by putting a closed circle at \(x = 1\). We then shade in all the numbers to the left of 1, to show that all numbers less than 1 are solutions. See the figure below.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is a closed circle on 1. The number line to the left of 1 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c89f24114c86a88a457ff1ee55c0119e4aea0a91"/></media></figure></para>
<para>There is no lower end to those numbers. We write \( x ≤ 1 \) in interval notation as (−∞,1]. We use the left parenthesis symbol, (, to show that the endpoint of the inequality is not included. The right bracket symbol, }, shows that the endpoint is included. The symbol −∞ is read as “negative infinity.” </para>
<para>The figure below shows both the number line and interval notation.
</para>
<title>Inequalities, Number Lines, and Interval Notation</title>
<newline/><div><emphasis><figure><media alt="Four different number lines are shown."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ae108513af5043e10799df8d1eed5b40269dbece"/></media></figure></emphasis></div>

<title>Try It: Graphing Linear Inequalities</title>
<para>For questions 1 - 2 use the following inequality:</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>\( x &gt; -3 \)</para>
</blockquote>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write the inequality on a number line.
    <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis></para>
    <figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on &#10;–3. The number line to the right of –3 is highlighted.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/10effd40cd28ca12937e70f7415e5166edad0307"/></media></figure></item>
  <item>Write the inequality in interval notation.
    <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\((-3, ∞)\)</para>
  </item>
</list>
<para>For questions 3 and 4 use the following inequality:</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>\(  x ≤ 5 \)</para>
</blockquote>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write the inequality on a number line.
    <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis></para>
    <figure><media alt=" A number line is shown. There is an closed circle &#10;on 5. The number line to the left of 5 is highlighted.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b549d66de4f02bdbf801752a89ee06481e8d9689"/></media></figure></item>
  <item>Write the inequality in interval notation.
    <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\((∞, 5]\)</para>
  </item>
</list>
</section>
